92186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166915 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1045 "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167013 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "be95ee71-ebc4-546c-a738-6a6db824342c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167200 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6644c70-dc61-58f2-81c3-a23eae94c4cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167200 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/09/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167207 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167207 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f707130-0164-506c-911a-bb075d503cd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167207 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/f5/f54544342b11a60ec784160f582b5bdd03a28abee01767a732bef6ac03d9ec62.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{2t})^{′}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}}{2{c}_{1}{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167215 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167215 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167215 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7525f92-11a2-54b3-8c7c-6d2f543e65d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167215 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/37/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167217 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167217 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167217 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167217 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/03/039bae0d2eedf7d12d8283b2b134855c531efcba1bd0fa14fe9f841b28d5204e.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167217 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3495d1a5-5d34-547b-9b7d-bfc79add2594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167217 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167843 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167843 #edb/ref 1061 588112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167843 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a60bbebf-7d50-57b0-b823-f5cde68f6ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167843 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167844 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167844 #edb/ref 1061 588113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167844 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "752fcd7d-e772-51d0-84f5-e0724d157464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167844 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167845 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167845 #edb/ref 1061 588114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167845 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ed4db19-c2d0-5c82-a414-637905d1a91d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167845 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167846 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167846 #edb/ref 1061 588115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167846 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0849e9c4-6231-5416-bf0c-d15d2ba66f0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167846 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167847 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167847 #edb/ref 1061 588118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167847 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d06d3f5b-d8b3-5195-a3ad-20d231c0a7e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167847 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167848 #edb/ref 1061 588121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d22549d-343c-5238-823d-0fdd3d6b11cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167849 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167849 #edb/ref 1061 588122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167849 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "edbdd357-26e5-5671-9724-72c90fe9512d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167849 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167850 #edb/ref 1061 588125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8abcadd-58fc-5f3e-a1a9-f6f32aaa7405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167850 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167851 #edb/ref 1061 588126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167851 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cadc020-1f0c-5e3d-88b7-c3977e63cc74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167851 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167852 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167852 #edb/ref 1061 588184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167852 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cfc4842-67bf-5d76-b1c9-2607d80a8a6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167852 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167960 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167960 #edb/ref 1061 608071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167960 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ee8907e9-ae68-5313-b486-9d33968f5dc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167960 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167961 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167961 #edb/ref 1061 608072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167961 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "78ab8590-2a54-5795-87c0-331930a7d959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167961 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167997 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a *decoupled* linear system of ODEs\n![](images/3f/3f7a114fa75100f8cd3ccf72822758e668f3a6a0824ad5f5437ebef138928fb5.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we consider only decoupled systems with nonzero eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/b8/b8ed089264b88bdcf4018f44db6dade448063b56a6ded558fb74d7c2ca61c0de.svg) so the origin is the equilibrium point we will classify.\n\nRecall that the eigenvalues for a diagonal matrix are its diagonal entries, ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/af/afb536c2c395100d85543d9c25f58d867e0bcfbd5f826d794284b648e7cc0550.svg) \nThe corresponding eigenvectors are parallel to the coordinate axes:\n![](images/83/8337ebfc4aae4b8e97438cdba58872082bdc8965932659fdf2f8ff9cf061b375.svg)\n\nSo, the general solution of the system is given by\n![](images/fc/fcb5c1cea49dfe7aa1058f64d859a8b47c363f7f6195bf68056b18f1fa6d37f2.svg)\n\nNotice that if a solution starts on one of the coordinate axes, it remains on that axis. These are called **straight-line solutions**:\n\n- On the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis (![](images/8d/8da7ef7a61d4c5af4d28575fcd263714cf3bda8e7d94985bd4bea5c03eb2d53f.svg)): \n![](images/aa/aa9cd3c49609ee6d09a61c9c31438e4297620feedfda1073fbc55c08e7c53d01.svg)\n\n- On the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis (![](images/f2/f271ee76900762067d886b30871a9ea7538ae69318ad4514e5b39cfdce12644b.svg)):\n![](images/50/509154a662b215d445b282396a8f43828e582d43282467a0da73843ade7ec5d8.svg)\n\nThe stability of these solutions as ![](images/91/919e9a7f2e074404c564846d6d8e1576e63b42ecbcc768b97d0632c645e32f22.svg) depends on the *signs* of the eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/af/afb536c2c395100d85543d9c25f58d867e0bcfbd5f826d794284b648e7cc0550.svg) We will explore the three main stability cases in the upcoming examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167997 #edb/ref 1065 14436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167997 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3ab2b715-abcc-511c-bbda-a2664d39efda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167997 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168001 #edb/ref 1064 "For a decoupled system\n![](images/df/df904ec5214399987c94f99323d3c3c4c06d9e19242c0d580574488011475acc.svg)\nwe know the *signs* of ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) tell us the type of the equilibrium (sink/source/saddle). But how can we determine the shape of trajectories in cases when the origin is a source (![](images/bb/bb6302eab08feb0614c62d4ea8012676675715a469dd30a7321ec228ff7ef4e9.svg)) or a sink (![](images/be/be8860e40a1c229262d3bf3375732f4b4583cad7aaf63f3a17e871bbd5a51abb.svg))?\n\nTo do this, we can compare the rates of change of ![](images/f7/f7dbfa03976240534a1b57f2cc59c474401a0d9524bc5f627736da01a0d775c1.svg) and ![](images/cc/cc436bde605f3822acb66d36528ba42a812a50fdbea361b31f0dbd0cba61d810.svg) by looking at the slope of the tangent line to the trajectory, ![](images/c7/c7244a31f756199a29c0e7e755eccdf9274346e643e0856566469b35a207e53a.svg) Assuming ![](images/37/371aec72d3e326e3e52fbd1a1cfc06be06b33e3054e60b8c286a9fcbac39141e.svg) we have:\n![](images/be/be21baf0c518d85b105b0f569382fbc47075c268847437046b042db36a9b4b8a.svg)\n\nAs we can see, the long-term behavior of the slope as ![](images/91/919e9a7f2e074404c564846d6d8e1576e63b42ecbcc768b97d0632c645e32f22.svg) depends on the sign of the exponent, ![](images/7c/7cde6653feb562f0d136150bffa6f2925af0c7dbcb9ede5c7f1b3e0f10f3796d.svg)\n\nThis comparison is governed by which eigenvalue is larger. We call the larger eigenvalue (the one that is more positive, or less negative) the **dominant eigenvalue**.\n\nIn the next slides, we will analyze the source and sink cases separately." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168001 #edb/ref 1065 14456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168001 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "406ac21a-bfe3-5af2-bc34-8bb09ae11870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168001 #edb/ref 1067 "Dominant Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168002 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2>0.$ \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/75/75e7a0e16141db2a8da210c1ab30fc7328ccc98f861afec4db69d951ff3c9121.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/28/280e99a308962d43d1060be7100e294623d3bc3bfbe0579ab61d817695ad9e45.svg) Since $\\lambda_2>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *unstable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called an **unstable node** or **source**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw:\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory for an *unstable node* emanates from the origin toward infinity. \n\n![](images/c9/c9f315c0b8a60a2c00e2f8e8e8b954214ce4d82cfd5832e15c26b429e29e535e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168002 #edb/ref 1065 14460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168002 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "302544e8-9eeb-5087-ae71-7143ffcbad9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168002 #edb/ref 1067 "Both Positive Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168003 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n![](images/3e/3e1a6630e46ba96ca21946d9feedc452d8416fe5bac17d5e8275c41c489d19ea.svg)\nwhere ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg)\n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/a1/a12ab1d3c69a589aa8de66fe8d14ed19b2938a9ef5591d87860e101a95afdc55.svg) Since ![](images/3d/3db8d2103adafd2be29df482295b07ef63576be6f152793463e034e808725edf.svg) we have that ![](images/bb/bb6223675110a379d33203ff637afaa6aefd3edf507b5a93386c608aa7aafca5.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/aa/aae0f8015e0a575ca1e1fdbb37d011cbf78574649e3c9874163c3f14f1ca9929.svg) Since ![](images/23/23d0a8291d49cc1d2f0c6cff735a4d5214c2d9079dca67d803846af9b063358f.svg) we have that ![](images/69/69c464920e94e968ac5b2fe07f52d5e8a5e694f0fab6c9bfdcfd1b359a82e89e.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *stable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **stable node** or **sink**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory flows into the origin.\n\n![](images/11/119ac25f56cfb326a9eb39eaf63c41e8497147c80b14da3a38e557e8aeb4f905.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168003 #edb/ref 1065 14461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168003 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "98c957b9-817c-5bdd-9e7a-9680641d5c55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168003 #edb/ref 1067 "Both Negative Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168004 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a decoupled linear system\n![](images/e1/e1efdd0b8c037c01450042384ce3bac663d0b549fa9557d0db09b692754cab0f.svg)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous linear system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$ \nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst of all, the coordinate axes are straight-line solutions:\n\n- On the $x$-axis, we have $y(t)= 0$ and $x(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}$.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ the $x$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ the $x$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\n- On the $y$-axis, we have $x(t)=0$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ the $y$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ the $y$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168004 #edb/ref 1065 14462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6fd81402-072b-5676-9cac-f53af20f3174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168004 #edb/ref 1067 "Classification of Equilibria" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168020 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2<0$ (the opposite case, where $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2>0,$ is similar). \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/4d/4dc3aaf0f7dfd85070df84c13abe2cf02d85a704167569b3d3a76f2b58178c5b.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin. This is called an **unstable axis**.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/76/76f6b9bbcdff2c87295fb0cd14dbdbbaa5e6c75eeb49c1cc158a4e8bd3da059d.svg) Since $\\lambda_2<0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *toward* the origin. This is called a **stable axis**.\n\nBecause one axis is stable and the other is unstable, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **saddle point**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), while\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory bends from the stable direction and then peels away along the unstable direction.\n\n![](images/f2/f2538a59050e604d85fd9726e688cfbd2a5c9bdff4d15dd5ed465ed04f7290b4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168020 #edb/ref 1065 14565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168020 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8fe29b2e-2a4a-5a2d-aaea-49ed3c5a7c1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168020 #edb/ref 1067 "One Positive and One Negative Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168057 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's analyze the case where the origin is a source, with ![](images/b8/b89d2dea375e40cafca8a025742ea1ba94b01ff3dd5926dc21b62444847e237f.svg)  For a decoupled system with solutions ![](images/6b/6b0666459fb6515324b663cc73f0e9327d845f40fd7772aa8850a4f14b55239d.svg) and ![](images/e7/e78f66d899f375fcf9894ab58968f92123556325139411dd278712df59c7e0ce.svg) trajectories move away from the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/3c/3cda8c604691cfc8889d64007bce911c2ea6bd3ba06a7a14cb3adef0cacbccfc.svg) then ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/27/27aba369b910849246f3c1c32106bca7e1e83a6223410a2fdc233fdea0a7367b.svg) In this case:\n![](images/b6/b6ee7ac8d56a4a4a1cdfb29948285c86491fc884911de538b875420bcabf1b55.svg)\nas ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) This means the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories move away from the origin.\n\n- If ![](images/a5/a5bd23277d5f3a652a992839592d09551cd2278773b0f92cbdc06b247f69dc64.svg) then ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/b0/b0dbb3708b2ff42502115d98637093d3b96d1f3f4ee4f9393046d0c3396068a6.svg) In this case:\n![](images/fd/fd6de4580c33c90dcadc8599e19917522b63327c668e8fec6856eec61c7ba3a3.svg)\nas ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) This means the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as the trajectories move away from the origin.\n\nNotice that in both cases, trajectories become parallel to the axis corresponding to the *dominant eigenvalue* as they move away from the origin (the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis on the left, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis on the right).\n\n![](images/28/286b72736b47e9d318f585fc0ef0ae69e4150914c3ca7a8981d236d08305a6a5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168057 #edb/ref 1065 14895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168057 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "534a4770-a0a6-5b07-a36e-0bc08f985630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168057 #edb/ref 1067 "Unstable Node (Source)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168058 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's analyze the case where the origin is a sink, with ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg) For a decoupled system with solutions ![](images/6b/6b0666459fb6515324b663cc73f0e9327d845f40fd7772aa8850a4f14b55239d.svg) and ![](images/e7/e78f66d899f375fcf9894ab58968f92123556325139411dd278712df59c7e0ce.svg) trajectories approach the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/48/481eb665d12add01a80a4646ec2762c89c9a543bf87b959b246d469a450eca6d.svg) then ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/27/27aba369b910849246f3c1c32106bca7e1e83a6223410a2fdc233fdea0a7367b.svg) As ![](images/37/370259707c15937e566c781bb8f7aaf39643a5c059b246f2cec5477528eee994.svg)\n![](images/b6/b6ee7ac8d56a4a4a1cdfb29948285c86491fc884911de538b875420bcabf1b55.svg)\nThis means the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as trajectories approach the origin.\n\n- If ![](images/80/80105ab4e61c0e2a59d11da4b534753b3c811b424f1e0b82704c6e28faecf9b8.svg) then ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/b0/b0dbb3708b2ff42502115d98637093d3b96d1f3f4ee4f9393046d0c3396068a6.svg) As ![](images/37/370259707c15937e566c781bb8f7aaf39643a5c059b246f2cec5477528eee994.svg)\n![](images/fd/fd6de4580c33c90dcadc8599e19917522b63327c668e8fec6856eec61c7ba3a3.svg)\nThis means the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as trajectories approach the origin.\n\nNotice that in both cases, trajectories become parallel to the axis corresponding to the *dominant eigenvalue* as they move toward the origin (the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis on the left, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis on the right).\n\n![](images/b6/b665d5328ae362ada2208da2583fd90247078490145426072455d43f8d36227f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168058 #edb/ref 1065 14896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168058 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b95d431-e3ab-5635-b641-a019d23397b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168058 #edb/ref 1067 "Stable Node (Sink)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302486 #edb/ref 1080 "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302486 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302486 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04a2b290-8039-5ca4-94f1-b39d0c36e25a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302512 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302512 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 